Separate conservation from component laws and boundary conditions (@engineering-component-laws: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/conduction/Fourier-law-conductivity-and-a-local-heat-flux.lingua#r_05EQENX75RXRDXHYBA5PXC2A28") { r_00FBD936549250285648668BF1
Suppose a body receives power P and releases a heat rate H. Conservation gives dE/dt=P−H. It does not tell us E(T), H(T,T₀), the ambient temperature T₀ or the starting state. These are different inputs to the model: a storage relation, a transfer relation, a boundary condition and an initial condition. An algebraic solution can be perfectly consistent with an unsuitable component relation.

In a deliberately restricted thermal model, E−E_ref=C(T−T_ref) with constant positive capacity C measured in J/K. Write H=G(T−T₀), where conductance G has units W/K and is constant over the stated range. Then both C dT/dt and G(T−T₀) have units W. Capacity describes how much energy changes the state; conductance describes a transfer at a difference. A large capacity does not automatically mean a good steady heat path.

For a plane slab with constant conductivity k, area A and thickness L, assume one-dimensional steady conduction, no internal generation, maintained face temperatures and negligible contacts/side exchange. Fourier's relation H=−kA dT/dx and constant H give T_hot−T_cold=HL/(kA). Hence G=kA/L. With k=0.4 W/(m·K), A=0.03 m² and L=0.006 m, G=2 W/K; a 4 K difference gives 8 W. The coefficients are hypothetical. A real porous, anisotropic, changing or imperfectly contacting body requires evidence before this law is used. The shared Fourier Record follows with its own example and fuller reading.

Some other networks have similar equations while tracking different quantities. Electric charge q is a signed conserved property measured in coulombs. Current I is signed charge crossing a chosen surface per time, measured in A=C/s. In an electrostatic description, potential difference V is potential-energy change per charge, measured in volts, J/C. A stationary ohmic element can obey I=g(V₁−V₂), with g in A/V; its dissipated power is I(V₁−V₂), not I alone. Hydraulic volume flow Q_v can be modeled as h(p₁−p₂) in a specified linear viscous regime, with h in m³/(s·Pa); pressure-work rate is Q_v(p₁−p₂). Compressibility, elevation, inertia and nonlinear losses can invalidate that simple reduction. Incompressible volume balance derives from mass conservation and constant density.

A thermal edge H already has power units. H(T₁−T₂) has units W·K, so it is not the electrical power formula with renamed symbols. Mathematical analogy helps assemble equations; it does not identify physical quantities, validate a regime or transfer a material coefficient between domains.

**Read:** [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../../physics/electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q), sections 1.6, 7.2, 9.1 and 9.4 for conduction, potential, current and the empirical scope of Ohm's relation. [Charge, field, potential, current and energy are different quantities](../../physics/electromagnetism/foundations/Charge-field-potential-current-and-energy.lingua#r_73F672N5XQ9B4B1MPJAKCK2EGS) remains available for later field formation; the common route selects no whole electromagnetic course. State definitions before using the analogy, and keep the readings' more advanced exercises for their physical preparation.

**Practice:** classify each of M′=f_in−f_out, E=Cθ+E_ref, H=Gθ, T₀=constant and θ(0)=θ_i. Derive how doubling A or L changes the slab prediction under its assumptions. Give an observation that would reject constant G without violating conservation. Explain why fitting G and then checking the same fitted datum supplies little independent evidence.

